{"id":130,"date":"2026-07-09T14:28:15","date_gmt":"2026-07-09T14:28:15","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/localhost:8181\/guest-rooms-that-stay-booked-innkeeper-hvac-along-market-street\/"},"modified":"2026-07-14T19:43:54","modified_gmt":"2026-07-14T19:43:54","slug":"guest-rooms-that-stay-booked-innkeeper-hvac-along-market-street","status":"publish","type":"page","link":"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/guest-rooms-that-stay-booked-innkeeper-hvac-along-market-street\/","title":{"rendered":"Lewisburg&#8217;s Historic Board Says No to Your Condenser"},"content":{"rendered":"\n<div class=\"wp-block-cover alignfull s-hero-cover\" style=\"padding-top:3rem;padding-right:2rem;padding-bottom:3rem;padding-left:2rem\"><span aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-cover__background has-contrast-background-color has-background-dim-70 has-background-dim\"><\/span><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"697\" class=\"wp-block-cover__image-background wp-image-198\" alt=\"brick rowhouse street\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/10787664563_3d35f30f8e_b.jpg\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/10787664563_3d35f30f8e_b.jpg 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/10787664563_3d35f30f8e_b-300x204.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/10787664563_3d35f30f8e_b-768x523.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><div class=\"wp-block-cover__inner-container has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-cover-is-layout-ff0c90b8 wp-block-cover-is-layout-constrained\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center has-base-color has-text-color s-hero-cover__title wp-block-heading\">Comfort without compromising character<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center s-lead has-base-color has-text-color has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">Lewisburg&#039;s federal-style homes need HVAC solutions that respect preservation rules.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-buttons-is-layout-35f06ea7 wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"\/contact\/\">Get a System Design<\/a><\/div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-button is-style-outline is-style-outline--1\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"#content\">Learn More<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"s-trust-bar\"><div class=\"s-trust__item\">15+ Years Serving PA<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Licensed &amp; Insured<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Same-Day Emergency<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Local to Your Area<\/div><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--cream has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why federal townhouses demand different thinking<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The brick facades lining Market Street represent one of the most intact early-19th-century streetscapes in central Pennsylvania. These three-story federal townhouses\u2014narrow-fronted, with dormer windows and symmetrical details\u2014are protected under local preservation ordinances that restrict exterior modifications. You can&#039;t mount a condenser unit on the front wall or in a side yard visible from the public right-of-way. This creates a practical problem: where does the equipment go?<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Rear courtyards work if you have one. Rooftops are technically possible but expensive and rarely approved. The result is that many homeowners still rely on window units, fans, or accept that central air isn&#039;t compatible with living here. The good news: ductless mini-splits and thoughtful heating upgrades can deliver modern comfort while staying compliant.<\/p>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--sand has-tint-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--sand has-tint-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">HVAC solutions for historic homes<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide s-feature-row is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-794e3cfa wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"768\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/3145968830_73d42a7d2e_b.jpg\" alt=\"attic insulation\" class=\"wp-image-189\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/3145968830_73d42a7d2e_b.jpg 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/3145968830_73d42a7d2e_b-300x225.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/3145968830_73d42a7d2e_b-768x576.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--stock\" title=\"&quot;The R11 attic insulation&quot; by zieak, BY, via Openverse\">Stock<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Ductless mini-splits<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Rear-yard or courtyard condensers with indoor heads sized for each floor. Minimal building penetration, flexible placement, both heating and cooling.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"683\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-2-1024x683.png\" alt=\"technician - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1476\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-2-1024x683.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-2-300x200.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-2-768x512.png 768w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-2.png 1536w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gpt-image-1)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Radiator system upgrades<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Modern condensing boilers feeding existing cast-iron radiators or new baseboard. High-efficiency, silent operation, appropriate to the building&#039;s age.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-1.png\" alt=\"technician - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1755\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-1.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-1-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-1-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-1-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Compliant window units<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Rear-facing, seasonal installation with proper support and sealing. Lowest upfront cost, removable, works for single rooms or rental units.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-2.png\" alt=\"technician - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1756\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-2.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-2-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-2-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-2-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Rooftop systems<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Possible with vertical line-set routing and access provisions. Costly, requires Board approval, best suited to homes with deep setbacks.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--ink has-contrast-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text has-media-on-the-right\" style=\"grid-template-columns:auto 50%\"><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Condenser placement in preservation zones<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Lewisburg&#039;s Historic Architectural Review Board operates under guidelines that prioritize reversibility and minimal visual impact. Any exterior modification affecting building appearance from a public street requires Board review. Condenser units fall squarely under this jurisdiction because they&#039;re large, mechanical, and visually inconsistent with 19th-century architecture. The Board&#039;s preference is rear-yard placement, screened by fencing or plantings, at least 10 feet behind the rear wall.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This works in homes with deep lots and alley access. Many Market Street townhouses have shallow lots\u201420 to 30 feet total\u2014and the only access is through the house. Carrying a 200-pound condenser through narrow interior doorways and steep stairs is impractical, so placement often requires a crane lift from the street. This adds $600 to $1,200 to installation cost but keeps the equipment out of sight and makes the installation compliant.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Courtyard placement is the next-best option. Many federal townhouses have walled courtyards where a condenser can be positioned out of sight from the street. The courtyard walls are typically 6 to 8 feet tall\u2014high enough to screen a 3-foot condenser\u2014and proximity to the house keeps refrigerant line runs short and efficient. The challenge is drainage and airflow.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Condensers discharge hot air during cooling operation, and if the courtyard is enclosed on three sides with minimal ventilation, the discharged air recirculates and reduces efficiency. Positioning the unit so its discharge faces an open side of the courtyard solves this.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Condensate from the indoor unit also needs to drain\u2014either to a basement floor drain, a sump, or a drywell in the courtyard\u2014and routing condensate lines through thick brick walls requires core drilling and careful sealing to prevent water infiltration.<\/p>\n\n<\/div><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez3-condenser.png\" alt=\"condenser - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1794\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez3-condenser.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez3-condenser-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez3-condenser-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez3-condenser-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure><\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--warm-gradient has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text\" style=\"grid-template-columns:50% auto\"><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-radiator-1.png\" alt=\"radiator - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1775\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-radiator-1.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-radiator-1-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-radiator-1-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-radiator-1-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Radiator heating and boiler upgrades<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Most federal townhouses in Lewisburg were originally heated by fireplaces, later converted to coal-fired boilers feeding cast-iron radiators. Many radiator systems are still in place, though the boilers have been replaced multiple times. Modern condensing gas boilers are compact, efficient, and capable of delivering 95% AFUE, but integrating them with old radiator networks requires careful balancing.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The radiators were sized for steam or high-temperature hot water\u2014180\u00b0F\u2014and modern boilers often operate at lower temperatures to maximize efficiency. This means radiators may not deliver as much heat as originally, and rooms farthest from the boiler may run cool. The solution is upgrading the boiler&#039;s circulator pump, adding zone valves for room-by-room control, or replacing undersized radiators with larger units.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Boiler replacement in a federal townhouse typically costs $6,000 to $10,000 including installation, and venting is a major consideration. Older boilers used masonry chimneys, but modern condensing boilers vent through PVC horizontally through the foundation wall. The termination has to be at least 12 inches above grade and 4 feet from windows or air intakes, which can be difficult in narrow townhouse lots where basement windows are close to the sidewalk.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Baseboard hot water heat is common in homes where original radiators were removed during renovations. Baseboard is less visually intrusive than radiators but also less effective. Baseboard delivers about 500 BTUs per linear foot at standard temperatures, so a 10-foot wall with baseboard provides 5,000 BTUs. A single cast-iron radiator can deliver 8,000 to 12,000 BTUs depending on size.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Switching from radiators to baseboard often requires more linear feet of baseboard than available wall space. In small rooms or rooms with large windows, you run out of wall before meeting the heating load. The result is a room that&#039;s persistently cold in winter while the thermostat in the hallway says 70\u00b0F.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Fixing this requires adding supplemental heat or reinstalling larger radiators, often sourced from salvage markets or as expensive reproduction cast iron.<\/p>\n\n<\/div><\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--sand has-tint-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">What ductless systems cost and deliver<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide s-cards-2 is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-794e3cfa wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">System investment<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">$12,000\u2013$18,000 installed for a three-story townhouse, depending on number of indoor heads and line-set routing complexity. Both heating and cooling from one system.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Visual trade-off<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Indoor heads are visible\u2014plastic rectangles 10\u201330 inches wide, mounted 6\u20137 feet above the floor. Ceiling-cassette and floor-console options reduce visual impact but add cost.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Efficiency in winter<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Mini-split heat pumps lose efficiency below 20\u00b0F. Most homeowners retain their existing gas boiler as primary heat and use mini-splits for shoulder seasons and AC.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">No ductwork needed<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Eliminates need for hidden duct runs in brick walls. Each indoor unit requires only a 3-inch wall penetration for lines and condensate drain\u2014reversible if removed.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--cream has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">Questions about HVAC in Lewisburg&#039;s historic district<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-accordion s-accordion--bordered has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-1583283a wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Can I install a condenser unit in my front yard if screened by shrubs?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Probably not. Lewisburg&#039;s Historic Architectural Review Board generally prohibits condenser units visible from the street, even with screening. Plantings can be removed or die, and the Board&#039;s position is that mechanical equipment shouldn&#039;t be visible regardless. Rear-yard or courtyard placement is required.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>How much does it cost to add air conditioning to a federal townhouse?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Expect $12,000 to $20,000 for a ductless mini-split system serving a three-story townhouse, including outdoor condenser, indoor heads for each floor, refrigerant line routing, electrical work, and compliant installation. Costs increase if the condenser requires crane placement or if custom concealment is needed.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Are window air conditioners allowed in the historic district?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Technically no if visible from the street. Enforcement is inconsistent\u2014many homes have window units\u2014but the Board&#039;s position is that they&#039;re inappropriate and should be removed or relocated to rear windows. If you install one, choose a rear-facing window and remove it in winter.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>What if my radiators are original and I want to keep them?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">You can. Upgrading the boiler to a modern condensing model while keeping the radiators is common in Lewisburg. The radiators may need balancing or supplemental zones to heat evenly, and if they&#039;re damaged or undersized, replacement is sometimes necessary. A local contractor can assess them.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Do I need Historic Board approval for HVAC work?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Yes, if the work affects the appearance of the building from a public street. This includes condenser placement, roof penetrations, exterior venting, and visible line-set routing. Interior boiler replacement or ductless line sets routed only through the rear typically don&#039;t require approval, but it&#039;s wise to check with the Board before designing the system.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--ink has-contrast-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Balancing preservation and livability<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-indent has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Lewisburg&#039;s preservation rules exist to protect the streetscape that makes the town architecturally significant, and most residents understand that living in the historic district means accepting certain constraints. The challenge is that the constraints fall unevenly. Homeowners with deep lots, rear access, and financial resources can install compliant ductless systems with rear-yard condensers and enjoy modern comfort.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Homeowners in narrow townhouses with shallow lots, no alley access, and limited budgets end up with window units or no air conditioning at all. The preservation ordinance could be updated to allow more flexibility\u2014for example, permitting side-yard condensers with screening or exempting rear courtyards from review entirely\u2014but the Board has historically leaned toward preservation. For prospective buyers, HVAC should be part of the due diligence.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Walk the property and assess where a condenser could go, whether the lot has rear access, and what the current heating system is. These aren&#039;t deal-breakers, but they&#039;re realities of historic-district living, and ignoring them leads to frustration after closing.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--sand has-tint-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-diagnostic has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-b267b866 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:3rem;padding-right:3rem;padding-bottom:3rem;padding-left:3rem\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Historic home system health check<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Equipment visible from the street \u2014 may conflict with historic district requirements<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Original plaster showing hairline cracks near HVAC penetrations \u2014 vibration or moisture damage<\/li>\n\n\n<li>System hasn&#039;t been serviced in 2+ years \u2014 disrupt maintenance routines<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Basement damp year-round \u2014 stone and brick foundation moisture affecting equipment longevity<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Heating bills 50%+ higher than similar-size modern homes \u2014 envelope losses dominate<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--dark-gradient has-contrast-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-cta has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Ready to upgrade your Lewisburg home<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">We navigate Historic Architectural Review Board approvals, design ductless systems with concealed refrigerant routing, and position condensers in compliant rear-yard locations. Every project balances what&#039;s needed with what&#039;s allowed\u2014no shortcuts, no violations.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons